 {
  "id": "q-anselm-maius",
  "type": "quote",
  "era": "high_medieval",
  "unit": 11,
  "latin": "Et quidem crēdimus tē esse aliquid quō nihil maius cōgitārī possit.",
  "translation": "And indeed we believe that you are something than which nothing greater can be thought.",
  "context": "In his Proslogion, written around 1078 at the abbey of Bec in Normandy, Anselm speaks to God and defines him as the greatest thing that can be thought. From this he argued that God must exist in reality and not only in the mind, the reasoning later called the ontological argument. Maius, 'greater', is a comparative, and quō, 'than which', is an ablative of comparison. Possit is a subjunctive in a relative clause describing what kind of thing God is.",
  "more": "Anselm later became archbishop of Canterbury. A monk named Gaunilo replied that the same logic could prove a perfect island exists, and Aquinas, Descartes and Kant all took sides in the debate.",
  "person": "Anselm of Canterbury",
  "source": {
   "work": "Anselm, Proslogion",
   "citation": "2",
   "date": "about 1078"
  },
