 {
  "id": "q-cb-omittamus",
  "type": "quote",
  "era": "high_medieval",
  "unit": 11,
  "latin": "Omittāmus studia, dulce est dēsipere, et carpāmus dulcia iuventūtis tenerae!",
  "translation": "Let us leave our studies, it is sweet to play the fool, and let us pluck the sweet things of tender youth!",
  "context": "In this song from the Carmina Burana, a student urges his friends to put down their books and enjoy being young, since serious work suits old age. Omittāmus and carpāmus are hortatory subjunctives, 'let us leave' and 'let us pluck'. Dulce est dēsipere borrows a phrase from Horace, and carpāmus recalls his carpe diem.",
  "more": "Later verses invite the singer's friends down to the town squares to watch the girls dancing. The poem is number 75 in the collection.",
  "person": "Carmina Burana (anonymous)",
  "source": {
   "work": "Carmina Burana",
   "citation": "75.1",
   "date": "13th century"
  },
  "source_url": "https://la.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omittamus_Studia",
