 {
  "id": "q-kempis-operari",
  "type": "quote",
  "era": "late_medieval",
  "unit": 12,
  "latin": "Multa bona potes operārī dum sānus es, sed īnfirmātus nesciō quid poteris.",
  "translation": "You can do much good while you are healthy, but once you are sick, I do not know what you will be able to do.",
  "context": "This advice comes from the chapter of the Imitation of Christ on thinking about death: do good now, because illness may take the chance away. Operārī, 'to work, to do', is a deponent infinitive, passive in form but active in meaning. Dum sānus es, 'while you are healthy', is a dum clause, and quid poteris is an indirect question.",
  "more": "The same chapter tells readers to live each morning as if they might not see the evening. Books on the art of dying well, the Ars moriendi, were popular in the same 15th-century world.",
  "person": "Thomas à Kempis",
  "source": {
   "work": "Thomas à Kempis, De imitatione Christi",
   "citation": "1.23.4",
   "date": "about 1420"
  },
  "source_url": "https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/kempis/kempis1.shtml",
  "attribution_confidence": "verified",
  "attribution_note": "Classical Latin would put the indirect question in the subjunctive; the text has the future indicative poteris.",
