 {
  "id": "q-oresme-moneta",
  "type": "quote",
  "era": "late_medieval",
  "unit": 12,
  "latin": "Monēta siquidem est īnstrūmentum aequivalēns permūtandī dīvitiās nātūrālēs.",
  "translation": "For money is an instrument of equal value for exchanging natural riches.",
  "context": "Nicole Oresme, a Paris scholar and later bishop of Lisieux, wrote his Treatise on Money in the 1350s, when French kings kept debasing the coinage to raise funds. He argued that although the prince stamps the coins, money belongs to the community that uses it, so a king who alters it for profit takes what is not his. Permūtandī, 'of exchanging', is a gerund in the genitive, and here it takes its own object, dīvitiās.",
  "more": "Oresme advised King Charles V of France and translated Aristotle into French for him. Long before Copernicus he argued that a rotating Earth could not be disproved.",
  "person": "Nicole Oresme",
  "source": {
   "work": "Oresme, De moneta",
   "citation": "ch. 6",
   "date": "about 1356"
  },
  "source_url": "https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/oresmius.html",
  "attribution_confidence": "verified",
