 {
  "id": "q-petrarch-irascimur",
  "type": "quote",
  "era": "late_medieval",
  "unit": 12,
  "latin": "Vērīs reprehēnsiōnibus īrāscimur, vērīs laudibus dēlectāmur.",
  "translation": "We grow angry at true criticism; we take delight in true praise.",
  "context": "In 1345 Petrarch found a manuscript of Cicero's letters in Verona and was shocked by his hero's political quarrels. He wrote two letters to Cicero as if to a living friend: the first scolded him, and the second, quoted here, offers praise to soothe him. Īrāscimur, 'we grow angry', is a deponent verb, while dēlectāmur is an ordinary passive, 'we are delighted'.",
  "more": "In the same letter Petrarch calls Cicero the 'supreme father of Roman eloquence'. His hunt for lost ancient texts helped launch the Renaissance.",
  "person": "Petrarch",
  "source": {
   "work": "Petrarch, Epistolae familiares",
   "citation": "24.4",
   "date": "1345"
  },
  "source_url": "https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/petrarch.ep1.html",
  "attribution_confidence": "verified",
  "attribution_note": "The source sentence begins quoniam, \"since\".",
