 {
  "id": "q-asser-libris",
  "type": "quote",
  "era": "early_medieval",
  "unit": 12,
  "latin": "… et psalmōs et Saxonicōs librōs et maximē Saxonica carmina studiōsē didicēre, et frequentissimē librīs ūtuntur.",
  "translation": "… they have eagerly learned the psalms and Saxon books, and above all Saxon poems, and they make very frequent use of books.",
  "context": "Asser describes how King Alfred brought up his children at court: his son Edward, the future king, and his daughter Ælfthryth learned psalms and English poetry. Alfred also set up a court school where noble boys read books in both Latin and English. Ūtuntur, 'they use', is a deponent verb that takes the ablative, so librīs is ablative; didicēre is a shortened form of didicērunt.",
  "more": "Edward the Elder succeeded his father in 899, and Ælfthryth married the count of Flanders. Alfred wanted every free-born English boy to learn to read English first, and Latin afterwards.",
  "person": "Asser",
  "source": {
   "work": "Asser, Vita Alfredi",
   "citation": "75",
   "date": "893"
  },
  "source_url": "https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/asserius.html",
  "attribution_confidence": "verified",
  "attribution_note": "The sentence begins nam, \"for\"; the subject is Edward and Ælfthryth.",
