 {
  "id": "q-dig-operam",
  "type": "quote",
  "era": "empire",
  "unit": 13,
  "latin": "Iūrī operam datūrum prius nōsse oportet, unde nōmen iūris dēscendat.",
  "translation": "Anyone who means to study law ought first to know where the word iūs comes from.",
  "context": "These are the very first words of Justinian's Digest, taken from Ulpian's textbook for beginners. Ulpian goes on to derive iūs, 'law', from iūstitia, 'justice', and to define law as 'the art of the good and the fair'. Oportet, 'one ought', is an impersonal verb, and nōsse, 'to know', is a shortened perfect infinitive of nōscō with present meaning. Unde … dēscendat is an indirect question.",
  "more": "Modern linguists think the derivation runs the other way: iūstitia comes from iūs. Ulpian took the definition of law as ars bonī et aequī from the jurist Celsus.",
  "person": "Ulpian (Digest)",
  "source": {
   "work": "Digesta Iustiniani",
   "citation": "1.1.1 pr.",
   "date": "early 3rd century AD (Digest 533)"
  },
  "source_url": "https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/justinian/digest1.shtml",
