 {
  "id": "q-dig-licet",
  "type": "quote",
  "era": "empire",
  "unit": 13,
  "latin": "Nōn omne quod licet honestum est.",
  "translation": "Not everything that is permitted is honourable.",
  "context": "The jurist Paul, a contemporary of Ulpian in the early 3rd century AD, wrote this short rule, later placed among the general rules at the end of Justinian's Digest. The law sets a minimum, he implies, but decency asks for more. Licet, 'it is permitted', is an impersonal verb, and quod licet is a relative clause.",
  "more": "Elsewhere the Digest says that in marriages one should consider not only what is permitted but what is honourable (23.2.42). The apostle Paul makes a similar point in 1 Corinthians 6:12: all things are lawful for me, but not all things are helpful.",
  "person": "Paul (Digest)",
  "source": {
   "work": "Digesta Iustiniani",
   "citation": "50.17.144 pr.",
   "date": "early 3rd century AD (Digest 533)"
  },
