 {
  "id": "q-dig-suo-iure",
  "type": "quote",
  "era": "empire",
  "unit": 13,
  "latin": "Nūllus vidētur dolō facere, quī suō iūre ūtitur.",
  "translation": "No one is considered to act with malice who is using his own right.",
  "context": "This rule of the jurist Gaius protects someone who simply exercises a legal right, even if a neighbour suffers as a result. Roman jurists applied it to a landowner who dug a well on his own land: if that dried up a neighbour's spring, he could not be sued for malice. Ūtitur, 'uses', is a deponent verb that takes the ablative, so suō iūre, 'his own right', is ablative.",
  "more": "Vidētur here means 'is considered'. Later lawyers set limits to the rule with the doctrine of 'abuse of rights', for acts done only to harm a neighbour.",
  "person": "Gaius (Digest)",
  "source": {
   "work": "Digesta Iustiniani",
   "citation": "50.17.55",
   "date": "2nd century AD (Digest 533)"
  },
  "source_url": "https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/justinian/digest50.shtml",
