 {
  "id": "q-tacitus-leges",
  "type": "quote",
  "era": "empire",
  "unit": 13,
  "latin": "Et corruptissimā rē pūblicā plūrimae lēgēs.",
  "translation": "And when the state was most corrupt, laws were most numerous.",
  "context": "Tacitus pauses his Annals to sketch the history of Roman law, from the Twelve Tables to the flood of special laws in the late Republic, many aimed at individual men. His bitter conclusion is that the more corrupt the state, the more laws it had. Corruptissimā rē pūblicā, 'the state being most corrupt', is an ablative absolute, and the verb 'were' is simply understood.",
  "more": "Tacitus wrote the Annals early in the 2nd century AD, covering the emperors from Tiberius to Nero. Critics of over-regulation still quote the line.",
  "person": "Tacitus",
  "source": {
   "work": "Tacitus, Annales",
   "citation": "3.27",
   "date": "about AD 115"
  },
