 {
  "id": "q-xii-nox-furtum",
  "type": "quote",
  "era": "republic",
  "unit": 13,
  "latin": "Sī nox fūrtum faxsit, sī occīsit, iūre caesus estō.",
  "translation": "If he commits a theft by night, and (the owner) kills him, he shall be held lawfully killed.",
  "context": "This law of the Twelve Tables let a householder kill a thief caught at night without being punished. By day, a thief could be killed only if he defended himself with a weapon. Estō is the future imperative of sum, the 'he shall be' of Roman law, and nox is an old form meaning 'by night'. Faxsit and occīsit are archaic verb forms, later fēcerit and occīderit.",
  "more": "Cicero cites the law in his speech defending Milo on a murder charge. The Book of Exodus has a similar rule about a thief killed while breaking in at night.",
  "person": "Twelve Tables",
  "source": {
   "work": "Lex Duodecim Tabularum",
   "citation": "Table VIII.12",
   "date": "about 450 BC"
  },
